## Proof

Let $z = x + iy$ and write $f(z) = U(x,y) + iV(x,y)$ with $U(x,y) = u(x)$ and $V(x,y) = v(y)$, where $u, v$ are real-valued functions of a single real variable.

Since $f$ is entire, it is holomorphic on all of $\mathbb{C}$, so the Cauchy–Riemann equations hold everywhere:

$$U_x = V_y, \qquad U_y = -V_x.$$

Computing the partial derivatives:

$$U_x = u'(x), \quad U_y = 0, \quad V_x = 0, \quad V_y = v'(y).$$

The second equation $U_y = -V_x$ gives $0 = 0$, which is automatically satisfied. The first gives

$$u'(x) = v'(y) \quad \text{for all } x, y \in \mathbb{R}.$$

The left side depends only on $x$ and the right side only on $y$; their equality for **all** $x$ and $y$ forces both sides to equal a common constant. Since $u$ and $v$ are real-valued, this constant is real; call it $a \in \mathbb{R}$:

$$u'(x) = a, \qquad v'(y) = a.$$

Integrating,

$$u(x) = ax + b, \qquad v(y) = ay + c, \qquad b, c \in \mathbb{R}.$$

Therefore

$$f(z) = u(x) + iv(y) = (ax + b) + i(ay + c) = a(x + iy) + (b + ic) = az + \beta,$$

where $a \in \mathbb{R}$ and $\beta = b + ic \in \mathbb{C}$.

**Converse.** Any function $f(z) = az + \beta$ with $a \in \mathbb{R}$, $\beta \in \mathbb{C}$ is entire, and writing $\beta = b + ic$ gives $f(z) = (ax + b) + i(ay + c)$, which is of the required form $u(x) + iv(y)$ with $u(x) = ax+b$, $v(y) = ay+c$.

Hence the most general entire functions of the form $f(z) = u(x) + iv(y)$ are precisely

$$\boxed{f(z) = az + \beta, \quad a \in \mathbb{R},\ \beta \in \mathbb{C}.}$$

